    thinking: "**Analyzing Vercel Deployment**\n\nI\'m currently focused on how to make an Express server work seamlessly on Vercel. My understanding is that the usual `app.listen()` approach is transformed into serverless functions. I\'m now looking into how Vercel handles routing, as this is crucial for the deployment.\n\n\n**Clarifying API Routing**\n\nI\'ve clarified how `/api/*` requests are handled: they target a serverless function entrypoint (like `/api/index.ts`). I\'ve verified that Vercel auto-runs the Express app serverlessly with the exported instance. Also, `server.ts` is still serving both Vite and Express in local development.\n\nI\'ve also confirmed that a `vercel.json` file is necessary to explicitly route requests, providing crucial rewrites for the API and SPA client. Further, it specifies build commands and output directories to achieve the optimal setup. I\'m now certain the current `server.ts` requires a specific Vercel-compatible export of the Express app.\n\n\n**Confirming Endpoint Functionality**\n\nI\'ve confirmed `/api/health` will return the correct status and that all AI endpoints will work identically. The Vite SPA routes will function because Vercel deploys the static `dist/` files. The frontend\'s relative fetch calls will work in production, as Vercel and frontend share an origin. I also see no CORS restrictions due to the same origin, though we might still consider standard headers for additional safety with different testing.\n\n\n**Validating Build Configuration**\n\nThe existing `dist/server.cjs` build is confirmed; it\'s essential for compatibility with multiple environments, including Vercel. Also, the `GEMINI_API_KEY` is resolving correctly from `process.env`. I\'m now looking closely at `server.ts` and `api/index.ts` to structure `app` and ensure the server and API are cleanly structured.\n\n\n"
